# Syndra — Local Development Environment
# Copy to .env and adjust values as needed.
#
# This file is the DEVELOPMENT reference. Do not hand-adapt it for production —
# run scripts/gen-prod-env.sh on the production host instead, which mints random
# secrets rather than inheriting the shared literals below. See DEPLOY.md.

# --- Datastore ---
# Required by docker-compose.yml with no default: postgres refuses to start
# without it, and the backend's DB_DSN default is derived from it so the
# credential is defined once. Set DB_DSN explicitly only when pointing at a
# database outside the compose file (as the local-dev value below does).
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=syndra_secure_password

# --- Backend ---
DB_DSN=postgres://syndra:syndra_secure_password@localhost:5432/syndradb?sslmode=disable
REDIS_URL=localhost:6379
PORT=8080

# Host ports. The container ports never move; these are what the host publishes,
# and they exist because a machine running a second stack has to move them
# without editing docker-compose.yml — a tracked file the next deploy overwrites.
BACKEND_HOST_PORT=8080
UI_HOST_PORT=3000
SYNDRA_API_KEY=dev_auth_token_secret

# Demo seed defaults to "on in local-dev, off in live Zitadel mode". Set
# explicitly to force-enable (useful for integration tests against a staging
# Zitadel) or force-disable (useful for reproducing empty-DB behavior
# locally). Default (unset) = auto-detect based on ZITADEL_DOMAIN +
# ZITADEL_MACHINE_KEY_PATH. Accepts 1/true/yes or 0/false/no.
#
# Leave it unset unless you have a reason. Auto-detect already does the right
# thing in both directions, and turning it off later does NOT remove rows an
# earlier run wrote — the app keeps serving them, and the Basic/Advanced
# console shows a banner counting them. `make reset-demo-data` clears them.
SYNDRA_SEED_DEMO=
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000

# Base URL that Zitadel uses to POST to Syndra (Actions v2 target + event
# listener target). Read by zitadel/actions/{register,rotate}.sh when
# rendering the targets manifest. Required when running the registration
# or rotation scripts; not consumed at backend runtime.
# SYNDRA_EXTERNAL_URL=https://syndra.internal

# Path to migration files (relative for local dev, absolute for Docker)
MIGRATION_PATH=file://db/migrations

# --- Grant Expiry Scheduler (optional — defaults are sensible) ---
# Background worker that sweeps direct_role_grants with expires_at <= NOW(),
# emits LLDAP removal intents, hard-deletes the rows, invalidates user cache,
# and cascade-revokes derived Zitadel grants.
#
# Disable only when reproducing a stuck-grant scenario or testing the sweeper
# in isolation. Single-LXC deployment runs one instance; there is no replica
# coordination to consider.
# EXPIRY_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true
# EXPIRY_SCHEDULER_INTERVAL=5m
# EXPIRY_SCHEDULER_BATCH_SIZE=500

# --- Drift Reconciliation Scheduler (optional — defaults are sensible) ---
# Periodic target↔Syndra grant reconciliation. target_only grants become
# drift_items for operator triage; syndra_only direct grants re-enqueue through
# the outbox (missed-webhook replay). Also triggerable on demand via
# [Reconcile now] on /governance/drift.
# DRIFT_SCHEDULER_ENABLED=true
# DRIFT_RECONCILIATION_INTERVAL_HOURS=6

# --- Zitadel Propagation Outbox (optional — defaults are sensible) ---
# Tuning for the drain (services/propagation): a transient failure (5xx/429/408)
# requeues the row up to OUTBOX_MAX_RETRIES attempts before it is marked failed
# for operator inspection; a terminal row (applied/failed) is opportunistically
# pruned once completed_at is older than OUTBOX_RETENTION_DAYS.
# OUTBOX_MAX_RETRIES=5
# OUTBOX_RETENTION_DAYS=30

# --- Revocation Drain (optional — defaults are sensible) ---
# The one background drain in the system. Grants stay operator-gated on every
# target, because the drain rule protects a consent property and nobody should
# gain access without a human deciding to give it to them. A revocation confers
# nothing, and a queued revocation is retained access, so it must not wait on
# somebody opening the right page.
#
# It shares the operator drain's advisory lock, so the two never dispatch
# concurrently; a pass that cannot take the lock returns and the next tick tries
# again. Disable only for a deployment that deliberately wants every dispatch
# observed by an operator — the cost is that every revocation then waits for one.
# REVOCATION_DRAIN_ENABLED=true
# REVOCATION_DRAIN_INTERVAL=5m

# --- Add-ons (optional — none registered by default) ---
# An add-on is a target adapter in its own container, reachable only on the
# internal network with no published host port. Registration here is a
# deployment fact: an add-on named below appears to operators whether or not it
# is currently answering, because navigation must not move when a container
# restarts. Registration alone makes nothing callable — the backend reads each
# add-on's /capabilities manifest, refuses a contract version it does not
# support, and offers only the intersection of that manifest with its own
# operation policy, which no manifest can widen.
# ADDON_TARGETS=truenas
#
# The base URL MUST be https, on the internal network as much as anywhere else.
# A client's TLS settings are consulted only when a handshake happens, so an
# http:// URL means the client certificate is never presented and the private CA
# is never used — the registration would report auth=mtls while nothing
# authenticates the connection at all. A non-https target does not register.
#
# The host is the compose SERVICE name and the port is the add-on's listener,
# both as they actually are — `truenas-addon` on `:8443`. An earlier example
# here named a host that resolves nowhere on a port nothing listens on, and the
# only symptom was an add-on that registered and never answered.
# ADDON_TRUENAS_BASE_URL=https://truenas-addon:8443
#
# These are paths INSIDE the backend container. Compose mounts BACKEND_SECRETS_DIR
# (default ./secrets/backend) at /run/secrets for exactly this, so a file named
# below must exist in that host directory under the same basename.
# ADDON_TRUENAS_CLIENT_CERT=/run/secrets/truenas-client.crt
# ADDON_TRUENAS_CLIENT_KEY=/run/secrets/truenas-client.key
# ADDON_TRUENAS_CA_CERT=/run/secrets/addon-ca.crt
# All three of the above are required together. Without the private CA the
# client verifies the add-on against the system root store, which authenticates
# an internal service by the public web PKI — a different and wrong trust
# anchor, not a weaker version of the right one.
#
# Or, where mTLS is impractical, signed requests carrying a timestamp and a
# body hash. Configure one mode or the other: a target with neither does not
# register at all, because calling the component that holds the NAS credential
# over an unauthenticated channel is worse than not having it. A bare shared
# secret is not a third option — it authenticates the caller but binds nothing
# to the request, so an intercepted call replays verbatim.
#
# Signing a request says nothing about the response and nothing about
# confidentiality, so signed mode still runs over TLS. Pair it with
# ADDON_TRUENAS_CA_CERT to verify the add-on against the private CA; on its own
# the CA is not incomplete mTLS and draws no warning. Without it the system
# roots are used, which is weaker but is a real check rather than none.
# ADDON_TRUENAS_SIGNING_KEY=/run/secrets/truenas-signing.key
# ADDON_MANIFEST_REFRESH_INTERVAL=15m
#
# The plan gate's two bounds, both read by the backend and neither previously
# written down anywhere.
#
# PLAN_COHORT_LIMIT is how many subjects a change may MOVE before the operator
# is asked to say the number out loud. It counts what would change, not what was
# selected — a selection of two hundred that grants three of them a role is a
# small change, and refusing it would teach operators to acknowledge everything.
# Default 25.
# PLAN_COHORT_LIMIT=25
#
# PLAN_LIFETIME is how long a confirmed plan may be cited before it has to be
# recomputed. The window an operator has to read a diff and approve it, not a
# session length. Default 15m.
# PLAN_LIFETIME=15m
#
# Bounds an operator's own use of an add-on operation, per member per hour. It
# exists because these are one-shot events that cannot be retried, so a script
# looping over a roster is indistinguishable from an operator working through
# one. Default 10.
# ADDON_MEMBER_OP_LIMIT=10

# --- The TrueNAS add-on container (docker compose --profile truenas) ---
# The other end of the block above: those variables tell the BACKEND how to
# reach the add-on, and these configure the add-on itself. Without them
# `docker compose --profile truenas up` stops at "TRUENAS_URL is required".
#
# The NAS, and the credential the add-on holds. A dedicated local TrueNAS user
# granted ACCOUNT_WRITE and SYSTEM_AUDIT_READ and nothing more.
#
# ACCOUNT_WRITE INCLUDES DELETION. TrueNAS requires exactly that role for
# user.delete and offers no narrower one, so the separate key the backend
# injects into purge is an audit and blast-radius separation — deletion stays
# out of the long-lived session and every one is traceable to a credential
# issued for that single call — and NOT a capability the standing key lacks.
# This block said the opposite for a while, which is the worse failure: a status
# invites a check and a plausible reason ends one.
# wss, always. TrueNAS REVOKES a user-linked API key presented over plaintext
# transport, so a ws:// here does not fail with an auth error you can retry — it
# destroys the credential.
#
# What the name has to earn is a certificate the add-on can verify, or
# TRUENAS_VERIFY_TLS has to go false. Simplest is an ACME certificate on the NAS
# itself and this name pointed straight at it, which also keeps SMB and NFS on
# the same name — neither can traverse an HTTP reverse proxy. See DEPLOY.md 5a.
# TRUENAS_URL=wss://nas.example.org/api/current
# ADDON_API_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/truenas-addon/api.key
# TRUENAS_API_KEY=            # the value inline, where a mounted file is impractical
#
# The name a MEMBER types into a file manager. Frequently NOT the middleware
# endpoint above and occasionally not the same machine, so it is never derived
# from TRUENAS_URL — SMB is published where SMB is published. Unset, the
# manifest omits its connection block and the member's page omits the mount
# instructions rather than printing a host that does not answer.
#
# In particular it is NOT the reverse-proxy name from TRUENAS_URL. The proxy
# terminates HTTP; SMB is tcp/445 and does not pass through it, so a member
# following instructions naming the proxy would be told to mount a host that
# answers on 443 and nothing else. Name the NAS itself.
# TRUENAS_SHARE_HOST=nas.makerspace.internal
#
# Surfaced through /health so an operator sees expiry coming. A silently expired
# key looks exactly like an outage.
# TRUENAS_API_KEY_EXPIRES_AT=2027-01-31T00:00:00Z
#
# TrueNAS ships a self-signed certificate, so a lab deployment sets this false.
# It defaults true and turning it off must be deliberate.
# TRUENAS_VERIFY_TLS=true
# TRUENAS_SUPPORTED_MAJORS=25.04,25.10,26.04
#
# active | draining | read_only. Draining is what makes an API-key rotation or a
# target upgrade safe: it finishes what is in flight and refuses new mutations.
# TRUENAS_LIFECYCLE_STATE=active
#
# Rotation bounds for the add-on's mutation log. Defaulted in the add-on; set
# them here only to size the named volume against something other than the
# default 8 MiB across 24 files (a ~192 MB ceiling).
# TRUENAS_MUTATION_LOG_MAX_BYTES=
# TRUENAS_MUTATION_LOG_KEEP=
#
# How the add-on authenticates the backend. EXACTLY ONE, and neither has a
# default: configuring both is refused at startup, because a caller would
# choose and the caller is the thing being authenticated.
#
# Mutual TLS — the stronger mode. The CA here is the same private CA that
# issued the certificate ADDON_TRUENAS_CLIENT_CERT presents.
# ADDON_CLIENT_CA_FILE=/run/secrets/truenas-addon/ca.crt
#
# Or signed requests. A PATH, like ADDON_TRUENAS_SIGNING_KEY above, and it must
# name THE SAME FILE: the two ends held this in two forms for a while, one
# HMACing the file's contents and the other the literal path string, and the
# only symptom was "no matching signature".
# ADDON_SIGNING_KEY_FILE=/run/secrets/truenas-addon/signing.key
#
# Where the four files above are mounted from on the host.
# ADDON_SECRETS_DIR=./secrets/truenas-addon
#
# And where the BACKEND's half is mounted from — its client certificate and the
# CA that authenticates the add-on to it. A separate directory on purpose: the
# add-on is the least trusted container in the deployment, and the credential
# that authenticates the backend TO it is not one it should be able to read.
# BACKEND_SECRETS_DIR=./secrets/backend

# --- Frontend (UI) ---
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8080
BACKEND_URL=http://localhost:8080
# SYNDRA_API_KEY is shared with backend above
# HMAC key for signing the session cookie (SC4). Optional — falls back to
# SYNDRA_API_KEY. Set a dedicated value to decouple cookie integrity from the
# backend API key rotation; without it, rotating SYNDRA_API_KEY logs every
# operator out. Required in production (gen-prod-env.sh always writes one).
# SESSION_SECRET=

# --- Zitadel OIDC (optional — uncomment to enable) ---
# ZITADEL_DOMAIN=your-instance.zitadel.cloud
# ZITADEL_AUDIENCE=your-pkce-client-id
# ZITADEL_CLIENT_ID=your-pkce-client-id

# Role key in urn:zitadel:iam:org:project:roles that grants operator access
# to the /api/v1/zitadel/* discovery and management endpoints. Default: "admin"
# ZITADEL_ADMIN_ROLE_KEY=admin

# --- Zitadel M2M / Management API (optional — uncomment for live orchestration) ---
# Service account key file for Management API access (user/grant/role CRUD).
# Add the service account as an org member (Organization > Members) with one of:
#   - ORG_OWNER                       (full access — recommended for PoC)
#   - ORG_USER_MANAGER + ORG_PROJECT_PERMISSION_EDITOR (least privilege)
#
# NOTE: These ORG-level roles cover the backend's runtime user/grant/role
# CRUD only. They do NOT cover Actions v2 target management, which is
# instance-scoped — `make zitadel-actions-register` will fail with HTTP 403
# on an org-only service user. If the same machine user runs the Actions v2
# scripts, additionally assign it the minimum instance-level permissions:
# `action.target.read`, `action.target.write`, and `action.execution.write`
# (Default Settings > Administrators). Only add `action.target.delete` if
# you intend to retire targets fully via the DELETE endpoint.
# Full matrix + narrowest-first assignment options: the "Service-Account
# Permissions" section of zitadel/actions/README.md
#
# ZITADEL_MACHINE_KEY_PATH is a HOST path (relative paths resolve against the
# docker-compose.yml directory). Compose bind-mounts this file to a fixed
# internal path inside the container — you never need to set that yourself.
# Leave unset to run in local-policy-only mode (no Management API calls).
# ZITADEL_MACHINE_KEY_PATH=./zitadel-machine-key.json
#
# Alternative to ZITADEL_MACHINE_KEY_PATH: a pre-minted M2M access token.
# Useful for CI runs where the key file is inconvenient to mount. The
# action-registration scripts prefer this when set; otherwise they mint a
# token from the key file via `go run ./backend/cmd/syndra-token`. The Go
# backend itself does NOT read this env var — it always mints from the key
# path via the zitadel package.
# ZITADEL_M2M_TOKEN=
#
# When BOTH ZITADEL_DOMAIN and ZITADEL_MACHINE_KEY_PATH are set, the backend's
# directory layer (backend/internal/directory/) flips to live-mode: user,
# project, and role dropdowns throughout the admin UI read from the Zitadel
# Management API instead of the hardcoded demo catalog. Look for the startup
# log line `[DIRECTORY] Source=zitadel` to confirm. A 30s TTL cache limits
# upstream calls; mutations from the /api/v1/zitadel/* handlers invalidate
# the cache targeted at the affected project.

# --- Zitadel Actions v2 signing key (optional — uncomment when Action target is live) ---
# Returned ONCE by Zitadel when the Action target is created (see
# zitadel/actions/register.sh). Capture from the response and inject here.
# When unset, the /api/action/inject endpoint runs in dev mode (signature
# verification disabled, warning logged). When set, every Action request
# MUST carry a valid ZITADEL-Signature header or receive 401.
# ZITADEL_ACTION_SIGNING_KEY=the-hex-key-zitadel-returned-at-target-creation

# When the signing key was last rotated. After each rotation, rotate.sh writes
# this (alongside the new key) to zitadel/actions/.action-env.fragment; apply
# via `cat zitadel/actions/.action-env.fragment >> .env` rather than copy-paste.
# On a fresh install before any rotate, set it to the install time directly.
# Read by GET /api/v1/zitadel/action-rotation-status and the /zitadel UI
# rotation panel. Format: RFC3339 UTC (e.g. 2026-04-24T12:34:56Z). When unset,
# malformed, or in the future, the rotation-status endpoint reports status=unknown.
# ZITADEL_ACTION_SIGNING_KEY_ROTATED_AT=2026-04-24T12:34:56Z

# Age (in days) at which the rotation-status endpoint flips from "ok" to
# "warn". Beyond 2x this value, status becomes "stale". Default: 90 days
# (common compliance cadence). Set to your organization's policy value.
# ZITADEL_ACTION_SIGNING_KEY_ROTATION_THRESHOLD_DAYS=90

# --- Zitadel Actions v2 event-listener signing key (uncomment when Action target is live) ---
# Returned ONCE by Zitadel when the syndra-event-listener Action target is created
# (see zitadel/actions/register.sh). Captured to .action-signing-key.syndra-event-listener
# and the .action-env.fragment apply file.
# When unset, the /api/webhooks/zitadel endpoint runs in dev mode (signature
# verification disabled, warning logged). When set, every event POST MUST carry
# a valid ZITADEL-Signature header or receive 401.
# ZITADEL_EVENT_SIGNING_KEY=the-hex-key-zitadel-returned-at-event-target-creation
#
# rotate.sh writes ZITADEL_EVENT_SIGNING_KEY_ROTATED_AT to the env fragment
# whenever the syndra-event-listener target is rotated, but no backend
# endpoint consumes it yet — the /zitadel Rotation Status panel currently
# reports only ZITADEL_ACTION_SIGNING_KEY_ROTATED_AT. Forward-compat only.
# ZITADEL_EVENT_SIGNING_KEY_ROTATED_AT=2026-04-24T12:34:56Z

# --- Self-mutation loop guard ---
# Zitadel service-user ID that Syndra uses for M2M Management API calls. Events
# whose editorUserId matches are dropped at the webhook listener (otherwise
# backend-initiated grant mutations echo through Actions v2 and re-trigger
# orchestration). Find via the Zitadel event log after the first M2M call.
# Unset disables the guard (dev-mode only).
# ZITADEL_M2M_USER_ID=

# --- Sync Service / LLDAP ---
# Removed. The sync service reflected Zitadel identity state into LLDAP for
# legacy protocols, and Syndra reaches those targets through their own
# management APIs now — see `addons/` and change `addon-platform`. Every
# LLDAP_* and SYNC_* variable that stood here is gone; nothing reads them.
